3. STRICTLY PROHIBITED ACTIVITIES
========================================================================

Except as expressly and unambiguously granted by prior Written
Authorization from the Author, You are strictly and absolutely
forbidden from performing any of the following activities with respect
to the Software, in whole or in part, in original or modified form,
in any medium, and by any means:

3.1  COPYING AND DISTRIBUTION
     (a) Copying the Software, in whole or in part;
     (b) Reproducing the Software on any medium (physical or digital);
     (c) Distributing, redistributing, publishing, or making available
         the Software to any third party;
     (d) Hosting the Software on any server, cloud service, container
         registry, or code-hosting platform;
     (e) Mirroring, forking, or creating derivative repositories;
     (f) Sharing the Software via file-transfer services, peer-to-peer
         networks, or any other means.

3.2  USE AND EXECUTION
     (a) Installing, deploying, or executing the Software in any
         environment (production, staging, development, test,
         laboratory, personal, corporate, governmental);
     (b) Using the Software as a component of any other software,
         service, platform, or system;
     (c) Using the Software to provide services to third parties
         (managed services, SaaS, MSSP, consulting, auditing);
     (d) Using the Software in any commercial, professional, or
         revenue-generating activity;
     (e) Using the Software to scan, monitor, analyze, or interact
         with any system, network, or asset that You do not
         personally own or that You are not explicitly authorized
         to test.

3.3  MODIFICATION AND DERIVATIVE WORKS
     (a) Modifying, altering, adapting, or translating the Software;
     (b) Creating derivative works based on the Software;
     (c) Merging the Software with any other work;
     (d) Extracting components, modules, algorithms, formulas,
         data models, or architectural patterns for reuse
         elsewhere;
     (e) Rewriting the Software in another programming language
         while preserving its structure, logic, or design.

3.4  REVERSE ENGINEERING
     (a) Reverse-engineering, disassembling, or decompiling the
         Software;
     (b) Attempting to derive the source code from binary
         representations;
     (c) Analyzing the Software to reproduce its functionality or
         behavior in a distinct implementation.

3.5  ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AND DATA MINING
     (a) Ingesting, embedding, or incorporating the Software into
         any dataset, corpus, or training set;
     (b) Using the Software to train, fine-tune, evaluate, benchmark,
         or improve any machine-learning model, large language
         model, neural network, artificial intelligence agent, or
         automated code-generation system;
     (c) Automated crawling, scraping, or indexing of the Software
         beyond what is strictly necessary for a search engine to
         display standard result snippets;
     (d) Vectorization, tokenization, or any pre-processing of the
         Software for AI purposes.

3.6  ACADEMIC AND COMMERCIAL EXPLOITATION
     (a) Submitting the Software (or any derivative) as academic
         work at any educational institution;
     (b) Presenting the Software (or any derivative) as Your own
         creation;
     (c) Publishing the Software (or any derivative) in journals,
         conferences, books, blogs, or presentations;
     (d) Selling, licensing, sublicensing, renting, leasing, or
         otherwise commercially exploiting the Software;
     (e) Filing patent applications on any invention embodied in
         the Software.

3.7  SECURITY AND MALICIOUS USE
     (a) Using the Software or any of its components (particularly
         but not limited to Nmap adapters, Hydra usage patterns,
         NSE scripts, attack simulation playbooks, and the
         Operation Nightfall scenario) against any system, network,
         or asset without the documented, prior, and unambiguous
         authorization of the owner of that system;
     (b) Using the Software as a tool of offensive cyber-security
         against any target;
     (c) Bypassing, disabling, or circumventing any security
         mechanism, authentication, or authorization control
         included in the Software;
     (d) Using the Software to conduct unauthorized penetration
         testing, vulnerability assessment, or red-team operations.

3.8  REMOVAL OF NOTICES
     (a) Removing, altering, or obscuring any copyright notice,
         author attribution, license notice, or trademark notice
         contained in or affixed to the Software;
     (b) Removing, altering, or obscuring the ARGUS visual
         identity, logo, or baseline;
     (c) Rebranding the Software under a different name.

